<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-indices-roots">
  <title>Index Form, Powers and Roots</title>
  <objectives xml:id="li-index-form">
    <title>Learning Intentions</title>
    <introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to do the following.</p></introduction>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Write repeated multiplication in index form, identifying the base, exponent and power.</p></li>
      <li><p>Evaluate integer powers and roots and understand their inverse relationship in appropriate cases.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the laws of indices to simplify expressions, taking care with zero and negative exponents.</p></li>
      <li><p>Distinguish a negative base from a negative sign outside a power, using correct grouping notation.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </objectives>

  <section xml:id="indices-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <p>In <m>a^n</m>, <m>a</m> is the <term>base</term> and <m>n</m> is the <term>index</term> or <term>exponent</term>. For a positive integer <m>n</m>, the expression denotes <m>n</m> factors equal to <m>a</m>. The resulting value is a <term>power</term>.</p>
    <p>Roots undo powers where appropriate: for example, <m>\sqrt{49}=7</m> is the principal square root, while <m>7^2=49</m>. The laws of indices express patterns in multiplying and dividing powers with the same non-zero base.</p>
    <p>Before using index rules, identify the base and distinguish a negative base inside brackets from a negative sign outside.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="indices-roots-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-indices-roots">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Simplify 2³×2⁴.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>2³×2⁴=2⁷=128 because powers with the same base add their exponents when multiplied.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-indices-roots">
      <statement><p>Evaluate (−3)² and −3².</p></statement>
      <answer><p>(−3)²=9, whereas −3²=−9.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
